verdict:
Most of round 2 holds up at source. Every ds4 M5 Max measurement, the AtomicChat fidelity rows, the GSQ task tables, the top-k paper numbers, the Artificial Analysis scores and every file size I re-summed match. Three claims need correction before section 1 can be trusted:
1. The Kimi K3 pruning summary ('more than 50% pruned keeps coding, loses facts') is wrong for the 80% build. It rests on single prompts and is confounded by 2-bit quantization in the 82% build.
2. The 5.85-10.6 GB/s streamer bandwidth behind the Kimi tokens/s estimate is an assumption carried over from Linux, stated as fact. The only macOS streamer the dossier quantifies implies about 3.4 GB/s.
3. GSQ is called CUDA-only, but its paper and repository support in-format GGUF K-quant refinement that runs on llama.cpp. That is a Mac-relevant route missing from G1, and Kimi GSQ needs a full 8-GPU node.

Eleven minor issues remain:
- the mgoin card's base scores (95.53 → 68.23 → 34.42) are omitted, though the dossier says the card was read in full;
- the event-atomic replay figure is inverted (it inflates misses, not hits);
- 'being downloaded' is stale, since the fetch completed in job 1528;
- an undocumented whole-prompt ds4 prefill assumption;
- the S137 truncation misdescription;
- residual checkpoint mixing in F1;
- an uncited 21.7 tok/s;
- unverifiable S131 numbers;
- several small citation errors.

No finding changes the headline that Kimi on this Mac means slow SSD streaming and that a fitting sibling such as V4 Flash 0731 or GLM-5.3-Flash is the practical choice. Round-1 fixes are confirmed for 19 of 23 findings, with CL-09's partial rejection justified; CL-04, CL-06 and CL-08 are only partly fixed.

